= 3
{parent=Section A(ii)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Read the straight-line extrapolation on the logarithmic dose axis, rather than the rounded shoulder of the measured curve. The retention and clearing intercepts are approximately $D_0=10$ and $D_{100}=50\,\mathrm{mJ\,cm^{-2}}$. Thus the <photoresist contrast> is
$$
\boxed{\gamma=\frac{1}{\log_{10}(50/10)}=1.43.}
$$
Here the subscripts denote the two dose thresholds used in the supplied definition. The physical positive-resist curve decreases with exposure; $D_{100}$ is the higher clearing dose in that definition, not a statement that the film retains its full thickness there. Graphical intercepts justify roughly two significant figures.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use <exposure dose> $D(x)=I(x)\tau$, with $\mathrm{mW}\times\mathrm s=\mathrm{mJ}$. At approximately $x=1.0$ and $3.0\,\mu\mathrm m$, the positive resist just starts to thin. The intensity there is about $5\,\mathrm{mW\,cm^{-2}}$, so
$$
\boxed{\tau=\frac{D_0}{I}=\frac{10\,\mathrm{mJ\,cm^{-2}}}{5\,\mathrm{mW\,cm^{-2}}}=2.0\,\mathrm s.}
$$
As a consistency check, the fully cleared region begins at approximately $x=1.5$ and ends at $2.5\,\mu\mathrm m$, where the intensity is about $25\,\mathrm{mW\,cm^{-2}}$. Multiplying by $2\,\mathrm s$ gives the clearing dose $50\,\mathrm{mJ\,cm^{-2}}$ at both edges.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A fully clear opening requires the higher clearing threshold, not merely the onset of thinning. At $\tau=20\,\mathrm s$,
$$
I_{\rm clear}=\frac{D_{100}}{\tau}=\frac{50}{20}=2.5\,\mathrm{mW\,cm^{-2}}.
$$
The intensity curve crosses this level at about $x=0.25$ and $3.75\,\mu\mathrm m$. Therefore the clear opening is approximately
$$
\boxed{w\simeq3.75-0.25=3.5\,\mu\mathrm m.}
$$
The longer exposure widens the opening because even the relatively dim image tails now accumulate enough <exposure dose> to clear the positive <photoresist>. The edge readings are approximate; interpolating a plotted curve does not support nanometre precision.

= iv
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

A **<negative photoresist> is removed at low dose and retained at high dose**. Its remaining-thickness curve rises from zero above a gel or retention threshold and approaches one above a larger full-retention threshold. A useful schematic model is
$$
f_-(D)=\begin{cases}
0&D\leq D_a,\\
\dfrac{\ln(D/D_a)}{\ln(D_b/D_a)}&D_a<D<D_b,\\
1&D\geq D_b.
\end{cases}
$$
The developed profile is $t(x)/t_0=f_-[\tau I(x)]$. A central intensity maximum therefore leaves a central ridge, with cleared regions towards the darker sides. **Halving exposure narrows and can lower the retained ridge; doubling it broadens the ridge and can raise its shoulders.** A saturated central plateau cannot become thicker than the original film in this simple model. If the half-dose peak is below $D_a$, no ridge survives; if it lies between $D_a$ and $D_b$, the centre survives only partially.

The negative-resist thresholds are not supplied, so there is no unique numerical profile or linewidth. The original schematic below declares its own illustrative thresholds and intensity function; it explains the dose mapping without treating a positive-resist curve as measured negative-resist data.

\Image[paper-1-resist-response.png]
{title=Negative-resist dose mapping and the effect of exposure time}
{description=Original illustrative model with declared retention thresholds. The intensity is a smooth test function rather than a copy of the examination graph; the numerical edge readings in the preceding parts come from the original PDF.}

= 4
{parent=Section A(ii)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take the ArF wavelength as $\lambda=193\,\mathrm{nm}$ and assume an **on-axis coherent illuminator**. A grating of period $P$ has diffraction orders with transverse spatial frequencies $m/P$. To reproduce amplitude modulation, the pupil must pass the zero order and at least the first spatial-frequency components. For symmetric on-axis illumination this requires $1/P<\mathrm{NA}/\lambda$.

For equal lines and spaces, the printed feature width is the half-pitch $w=P/2$. The ideal limiting values are therefore
$$
\boxed{w_{\min}=\frac{\lambda}{2\mathrm{NA}}=\frac{193}{1.2}=161\,\mathrm{nm}},\qquad
\boxed{P_{\min}=322\,\mathrm{nm}}.
$$
These are limiting values approached from within the pupil, with adequate mask contrast and a suitable resist; a weakly modulated mask need not supply sufficient image contrast. They are not the Rayleigh separation of two incoherent point objects. Off-axis coherent illumination can shift the admitted orders and changes this particular geometry.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The projection lens maps the mask's angular spectrum into its back focal plane: within scalar Fourier optics this is the **<Fraunhofer diffraction> or spatial-Fourier plane**. A periodic mask produces discrete diffraction orders, separated by $1/P$ in object-side spatial frequency after accounting for projection magnification. The finite <numerical aperture> bounds the transverse wave vectors admitted by the lens, so the pupil is a spatial-frequency filter.

For an ideal on-axis coherent system,
$$
\widetilde U_{\rm image}(\nu_x,\nu_y)=P(\nu_x,\nu_y)\widetilde U_{\rm mask}(\nu_x,\nu_y),
\qquad |\boldsymbol\nu|<\frac{\mathrm{NA}}{\lambda}.
$$
A second Fourier transformation forms the image, and the recorded intensity is $|U_{\rm image}|^2$. If only the zero order passes, the field is uniform and the line pattern disappears. Passing zero and first orders restores a periodic modulation; passing more orders sharpens the edges. Thus the loss of fine structure is explained by rejected diffraction orders, not by a geometrical inability to draw narrow lines. The field-transfer cutoff differs from the twice-as-large incoherent intensity-transfer cutoff.<Advanced Optical Imaging: Incoherent imaging by Delft University of Technology>[coherent and incoherent transfer: pupil filtering and intensity formation]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-grating-pupil.png]
{title=Coherent grating orders admitted or rejected by a finite pupil}

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Increasing the angular acceptance can collect higher spatial frequencies, but rays at large angles and away from the optical axis are harder to bring to the same focus. <Spherical aberration>, coma, astigmatism and field curvature can degrade contrast across a large exposure field. The <depth of focus> also decreases approximately as $\lambda/\mathrm{NA}^2$, making wafer height errors more important. A larger geometrical aperture is therefore not automatically better over the whole wafer field.

**<Immersion lithography> raises $\mathrm{NA}=n\sin\theta$ by increasing the refractive index of the medium between the final lens and wafer.** A suitable UV-transparent liquid permits a larger numerical aperture without increasing the ray angle by the same factor, easing the purely geometrical demand on the lens. Lens design and focus control remain necessary; immersion does not abolish aberrations. The liquid must also be compatible with the resist and free of bubbles and contamination, and absorption at $193\,\mathrm{nm}$ must be low.

= Section A(iii)
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= 5
{parent=Section A(iii)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use consistent units for the <Gibbs free energy>:
$$
\Delta G^\circ=\Delta H^\circ-T\Delta S^\circ
=-15.0-300(0.020)=-21.0\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Then $\Delta G^\circ=-RT\ln K$ gives
$$
\boxed{K=\exp\!\left[\frac{21000}{8.314(300)}\right]=4.53\times10^3.}
$$
This is a dimensionless thermodynamic <equilibrium constant> defined through activities. For an ideal dilute $1:1$ association, the numerical concentration association constant is about $4.53\times10^3\,\mathrm{M^{-1}}$ when concentrations use the $1\,\mathrm M$ standard state. Both favourable enthalpy and positive entropy favour association here; association need not always have a negative entropy change because solvent release can outweigh the loss of translational freedom.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<Cyclodextrins> are truncated-cone-shaped hosts that form <host–guest complexes>. Their hydroxyl-rich exterior interacts favourably with water, while the internal cavity is relatively nonpolar and can accommodate a suitably sized organic guest. The six-, seven- and eight-unit rings have different cavity sizes, so shape and size selectivity matter. Binding can involve <London dispersion forces>, displacement of cavity water and changes in solvation; no covalent modification of the guest is necessary.

An odorous organic guest can be included in the cavity, decreasing its free concentration and hence its tendency to enter the vapour phase. The same inclusion chemistry can increase the apparent aqueous solubility of a poorly soluble compound, and reversible association allows release when conditions or concentrations change. In a separation, guests with different association constants partition differently between free and bound states; a suitably immobilized host can therefore act as a selective stationary phase.

**The unifying mechanism is reversible molecular inclusion in a size-selective cavity.** These are chemical explanations of binding and partitioning. Biological action, therapeutic efficacy and physiological delivery mechanisms are outside the present nonbiology scope.

= iii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**The gas-phase attraction is a <cation–π interaction>.** The positively charged ion interacts with benzene's electron distribution and polarizes the aromatic molecule. An orientation above the ring face allows stabilization without a conventional covalent bond. A modest association constant reflects the balance of that attraction against the entropy cost of joining two independent species, at the stated gas-phase standard state.

In water, a small ion such as $\mathrm{Li^+}$ is strongly solvated. Forming a direct benzene complex requires rearranging or losing favourable ion–water contacts; benzene is also solvated, and the solvent screens electrostatic interactions. The relevant solution free energy therefore includes **desolvation and solvent reorganization**, not just the attractive energy of the isolated pair. The gas-phase association constant cannot be transferred to water without changing this thermodynamic cycle and the standard states. The interaction is not forbidden in solution, but it need not give appreciable direct complexation under the specified conditions.

= iv
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The receptor is a <triazolophane> with a relatively rigid central cavity. Its inward-facing triazole and phenylene C–H groups act cooperatively as <hydrogen bond donors> to an anion. The supplied preference is **$\mathrm{Cl^-}>\mathrm{Br^-}\gg\mathrm{F^-}>\mathrm{I^-}$**, so it is not a simple ranking by charge density or basicity.

Chloride has the best fit for several favourable C–H···anion contacts simultaneously; bromide is close enough in size to retain strong binding. Fluoride is too small to engage the whole donor array optimally, and its desolvation penalty can further oppose binding in solution. Iodide is too large for the cavity and cannot sit in the same favourable geometry. <Molecular preorganization> reduces the conformational penalty for binding a matched guest, but makes the cavity less adaptable to mismatched ones.

The exact solvent is not specified in this question, so a quantitative hydration argument would be unwarranted. The structure and observed nonmonotonic order support **size complementarity plus cooperative C–H hydrogen bonding**, with solvation modifying the measured constants. The original receptor study experimentally demonstrated halide size selectivity and tunability through the phenylene substituents.<Size-selective halide binding by Yongjun Li and Amar H. Flood>[2008, halide-binding investigations and cavity-size comparison]{ref}

= 6
{parent=Section A(iii)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take unit activity of each dissolved metal ion, unit activity of each metal, $p_{\mathrm H_2}=1\,\mathrm{bar}$ and $T=298\,\mathrm K$. The hydrogen-electrode <Nernst equation> gives
$$
E_{\mathrm H^+/\mathrm H_2}=-\frac{RT\ln10}{F}\mathrm{pH}
=-0.059117\,\mathrm{pH}\quad\mathrm V.
$$
Thus $E_H=-0.05912\,\mathrm V$ at pH 1 and $-0.82764\,\mathrm V$ at pH 14. For corrosion coupled to hydrogen evolution,
$$
\mathrm M+z\mathrm H^+\longrightarrow\mathrm M^{z+}+\frac z2\mathrm H_2,
\qquad E_{\rm cell}=E_H-E_M^\circ,\qquad\Delta G=-zFE_{\rm cell}.
$$
For alkaline solution the same electron balance can be written $\mathrm M+z\mathrm H_2O\to\mathrm M^{z+}+z\mathrm{OH^-}+(z/2)\mathrm H_2$, before allowing for metal-ion hydrolysis or complexation. Using $z=2$ for zinc and lead, and $z=1$ for lithium, the results per mole of metal are
$$
\begin{array}{c|rr|rr}
&\mathrm{pH}=1&\mathrm{pH}=1&\mathrm{pH}=14&\mathrm{pH}=14\\
\text{metal}&E_{\rm cell}\ (\mathrm V)&\Delta G\ (\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}})&E_{\rm cell}\ (\mathrm V)&\Delta G\ (\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}})\\\hline
\mathrm{Zn}&0.7009&-135.3&-0.06764&+13.05\\
\mathrm{Pb}&0.07088&-13.68&-0.6976&+134.6\\
\mathrm{Li}&3.0409&-293.4&2.2724&-219.3
\end{array}
$$
**Lithium is strongly unstable against hydrogen evolution in both media in this model.** The positive alkaline values for zinc and lead apply to the stipulated unit-activity free-ion products; they are not complete predictions of alkaline corrosion once hydroxide precipitation and complexation change the product chemical potentials.

= ii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**A useful aqueous <anode> must deliver the desired oxidation current while limiting parasitic corrosion and hydrogen evolution.** <Thermodynamics> sets the driving force, while <activation overpotential>, surface films and mass transport control the rate.

For <zinc>, the acid calculation favours dissolution with hydrogen evolution, but a substantial kinetic barrier to hydrogen evolution on a clean zinc surface can slow this loss. Impurities and catalytic inclusions can increase it. In alkaline electrolyte, zinc can be useful in batteries, but the unit-activity $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ calculation is incomplete: soluble zincate and zinc oxide or hydroxide alter the relevant equilibrium. A surface film can protect against corrosion or obstruct useful discharge, depending on its conductivity and transport properties.

For <lead>, acid corrosion has a much smaller driving force in the stipulated model, and slow hydrogen kinetics can permit practical use. The electrolyte anion matters: a lead sulfate layer in a lead–acid system has a different role from arbitrary soluble lead salts. In alkaline media, hydroxide formation, soluble complexes and <passivation> again need consideration before drawing a practical conclusion from the free-ion potential.

**Metallic <lithium> is unsuitable as an exposed anode in an ordinary aqueous electrolyte.** Its strongly favourable reaction with water overwhelms the argument for simply relying on a hydrogen overpotential. A lithium-ion host electrode is a different material and must be judged by its own operating potential and interfacial chemistry.

= 7
{parent=Section A(iii)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A conventional <capacitor> stores separated electronic charge across a dielectric, with capacitance set approximately by $C=\varepsilon A/d$. An <electric double-layer capacitor> stores charge at the interface between a porous electronic conductor and an ionic <electrolyte>. Electrolyte counterions screen charge on the electrode over a very small interfacial separation, and a porous electrode provides a large accessible area. The separator prevents an electronic short while permitting ionic transport; the two electrode interfaces form series capacitances.

**<Pseudocapacitance> is charge storage by fast, reversible Faradaic processes that give a capacitor-like relation between stored charge and potential over a useful range.** Examples include surface redox and suitably rapid insertion processes. It is distinct from purely electrostatic double-layer charging. Merely observing a redox peak does not establish pseudocapacitive behaviour: a slow two-phase battery reaction can remain battery-like even when its electrode is nanostructured.

= ii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For constant current and nearly constant capacitance,
$$
I=C\frac{dV}{dt},\qquad V(t)=V(0)\pm\frac{|I|}{C}t.
$$
An ideal <supercapacitor> therefore has **approximately linear charge and discharge ramps**, making a triangular voltage–time trace. Series resistance adds an immediate voltage step when current starts and a step of magnitude $2|I|R_s$ when it reverses directly from $+I$ to $-I$. Leakage and voltage-dependent capacitance introduce curvature.

A secondary <battery> instead follows electrode chemical potentials as its state of charge changes; many chemistries have broad plateaus or sloping plateaus, with polarization separating the charge and discharge curves. This distinction is typical rather than universal: some batteries have steeply sloping profiles, while strongly potential-dependent pseudocapacitive materials can depart substantially from a triangle.

\Image[paper-1-supercapacitor.png]
{title=Idealized supercapacitor and battery responses, with capacitance–potential alternatives}
{description=Original schematic curves; the lower panels distinguish a constant differential capacitance, a double-layer minimum and a localized redox contribution. They are alternative models, not a universal measured curve.}

= iii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Define mass-specific differential <capacitance>, or <specific capacitance>, by
$$
\boxed{C_{\rm sp}(V)=\frac1m\frac{dQ}{dV}},\qquad [C_{\rm sp}]=\mathrm{F\,g^{-1}}.
$$
State whether $m$ is one electrode's active mass, both electrodes' active mass or the entire device mass; these conventions are not interchangeable. An average capacitance inferred from a finite voltage interval is $\Delta Q/(m\Delta V)$ and need not equal the local derivative.

**A nearly ideal double-layer <supercapacitor> has an approximately flat capacitance over its stable operating window**, so stored charge rises nearly linearly with potential. Real electrodes need not be flat: dilute-electrolyte double-layer models can give a minimum near the potential of zero charge, while concentrated electrolytes, finite ion size and pore confinement can produce bell-shaped or double-peaked curves. <Pseudocapacitance> can add broad potential-dependent contributions near a redox transition. The sketch above therefore labels several possible behaviours instead of asserting a universal monotonic trend. Constant-potential simulations have directly found transitions between double-peaked and bell-shaped differential-capacitance curves as interfacial interactions change.<Insight into the Camel-to-Bell Transition of Differential Capacitance in Ionic Liquid-Based Supercapacitors by Zhongdong Gan and Yanlei Wang>[2022, constant-potential molecular-dynamics results]{ref}

= iv
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**Porous carbon emphasizes power and cycle life.** Activated carbon, graphene-derived structures and nanotubes can provide high accessible area, electronic conduction and relatively reversible double-layer charging. Cost and stability can be favourable. However, inaccessible micropores, tortuous ionic paths, poor packing density and surface functional groups can reduce useful rate performance or volumetric energy density. Area measured by gas adsorption is not automatically accessible to electrolyte ions.

**Metal oxides can add substantial redox charge storage.** A suitable oxide offers high <pseudocapacitance>, but its electronic conductivity, ion-transport distance and electrochemical stability can limit power. Some high-performing compositions are expensive or contain scarce elements. Structural changes and dissolution can shorten life. Conductive composites and thin active layers help, but adding inactive support dilutes device-level energy storage.

**Conducting polymers combine redox activity with electronic conduction and flexible processing.** Their oxidation state and counterion population change during charging. Repeated ion and solvent uptake causes swelling and shrinkage, which can crack or delaminate an electrode and shorten cycle life. Conductivity is also state-dependent, and potential windows are constrained by overoxidation or reduction. A practical comparison must include both electrodes, electrolyte, packaging and the usable voltage: $E\simeq CV^2/2$ makes voltage stability as important as a large gravimetric capacitance.

